\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[a4paper,margin=24mm]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,booktabs,hyperref}
\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}
\title{Cubic graphs with two octagons through every edge}
\author{A recovery of one lower bound in Goedgebeur--Jooken}\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
A graph is edge-girth-regular with parameters $(n,k,g,\lambda)$ if it is
simple and $k$-regular, has girth $g$, and every edge lies in precisely
$\lambda$ cycles of length $g$. We recover the lower bound
\[
                         n(3,8,2)\ge56
\]
from Table~1, physical page~17, of the fixed preprint
\emph{Exhaustive generation of edge-girth-regular graphs}, arXiv:2401.08271v2,
25 June 2024, by Jan Goedgebeur and Jorik Jooken. The reduction fixes one
radius-four neighbourhood and leaves only nine possible exterior graphs.
The final finite exclusion uses complete returned computations; no printed
source count is a completeness premise.

\section*{The forced neighbourhood}
For a cubic vertex $v$ with incident edges $e_1,e_2,e_3$, let $c_{ij}$ be
the number of octagons containing both $e_i$ and $e_j$. The equations
$c_{12}+c_{13}=c_{12}+c_{23}=c_{13}+c_{23}=2$ give
\begin{equation}\label{pair}
                         c_{12}=c_{13}=c_{23}=1.
\end{equation}
Consequently two octagons cannot share two successive edges. Every vertex
lies in three octagons, so the total number of octagons is $3n/8$ and
$8\mid n$.

\begin{lemma}\label{ball}
Every radius-four neighbourhood has spheres of sizes $1,3,6,12,21$.
Its edges incident with the first four spheres are determined, up to
an isomorphism fixing the root, by the model described below.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Girth eight makes the first three levels a tree. Each of the 24
nonbacktracking paths of length four from the root ends at distance four.
Indeed, a shorter route to its endpoint would give a cycle of length at
most seven. Two such paths with a common endpoint must diverge at the
root and have otherwise disjoint interiors, since any later divergence
or earlier reunion gives a shorter cycle. They form an octagon.

Three paths to one endpoint would yield three octagons. An internal
pair of successive edges on any one of the paths would belong to two
of them, contrary to~\eqref{pair}. Thus a repeated endpoint has exactly
two paths. The three octagons through the root give precisely three
repetitions, leaving $24-3=21$ endpoints. They use the three pairs of
root branches, once each.

Within a root branch, the two selected paths must separate at its first
vertex: otherwise they share the two edges beginning at the root.
Interchange the two children to label each path by its other root branch.
Within each of these six subtrees, interchange children to make the
selected path its first leaf. All these choices are independent.
This gives the claimed unique model. Every vertex at distance at most
three already has degree three, so no further edge can meet those levels.
\end{proof}

Here is a literal specification of the model $R$. Start with the root
$\varnothing$ and words $(i,\epsilon_1,\ldots,\epsilon_j)$, where
$i\in\{0,1,2\}$, $0\le j\le3$, and each $\epsilon$ is zero or one.
Join every word to the word obtained by deleting its last entry, with
$(i)$ joined to the root. For $i\ne j$, let $b(i,j)$ be the position of
$j$ in the increasing list $\{0,1,2\}\setminus\{i\}$, starting at zero.
For each unordered pair $\{i,j\}$ identify the two leaves
\[
                 (i,b(i,j),0,0),\qquad(j,b(j,i),0,0).
\]
There are 43 vertices and 45 edges. Its boundary $B$ consists of 18
vertices of degree one and three vertices of degree two. The only
cycles already present are the three prescribed octagons.

\section*{The nine exterior possibilities}
Lemma~\ref{ball} gives $n\ge43$. Divisibility by eight leaves only $n=48$
below 56. Suppose such a graph exists and fix its copy of $R$.
The exterior set $Y$ has five vertices. Its induced graph is a forest
of maximum degree three: a cycle contained in five vertices would
contradict the girth. If this forest has $e$ edges, the degree sums give
\[
 |E(B,Y)|=15-2e,\qquad |E(B)|=12+e,
\]
where the second expression counts the additional edges inside $B$.
Indeed $B$ has $18\cdot2+3=39$ remaining incidences.
These equations are useful checks; the enumeration does not assume
that they alone suffice for a completion.

There are nine possible forests up to isomorphism. The following table
uses labels $0,\ldots,4$ on $Y$. Each row records a complete zero-result
search. ``Plain'', ``edge order'', and ``neighbour orbit'' refer to the
three versions specified in the next section, not to distinct graph
classes. The node counts are execution checks, not labelled graph counts.
\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{c l l r}
\toprule
Row&Exterior edges&Search version&Visited nodes\\\midrule
0&none&neighbour orbit&965\\
1&01&neighbour orbit&8\,366\\
2&01,02&edge order&279\,872\\
3&01,02,03&edge order&57\,777\\
4&01,02,03,14&plain&44\,238\\
5&01,02,13&edge order&121\,973\\
6&01,02,13,24&edge order&35\,842\\
7&01,02,34&edge order&197\,402\\
8&01,23&neighbour orbit&6\,309\\\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The certificate also obtains this list independently by considering all
$2^{10}$ labelled graphs on five vertices, rejecting degree greater than
three and cycles, and identifying graphs under all $5!$ permutations.
There are 286 labelled forests and nine classes. This small exhaustive
list is checked against every literal target input.

\section*{Complete search and the two symmetry reductions}
For each forest $H$, the initial graph is the disjoint union of $R$ and
$H$. The permitted additional edges are exactly all pairs in $B$ and
all pairs between $B$ and $Y$. There are no other undecided edges.
In particular no choice of attachments, order of boundary vertices, or
interior forest is omitted by a preliminary numerical filter.

The search stores the current adjacency relation, degrees, and the
number $c(v;a,b)$ of octagons using the unordered neighbour pair
$\{a,b\}$ at $v$. All these counts are at most one. Before adding an
edge $xy$, it lists all simple paths from $x$ to $y$ of length at most
seven. A path of length less than seven rejects the edge. Each path of
length seven creates exactly one octagon. Its eight neighbour pairs
are added to the counts, and any repeated pair rejects the edge.
This includes repetitions between two octagons created by the same
new edge. Thus every rejection is necessary for girth eight and
\eqref{pair}; conversely the test detects every newly created forbidden
cycle or pair repetition. All new cycles contain the new edge, which
also proves that the stored counts are exact by induction.

At a recursive state choose a vertex $x$ of degree less than three.
The implementation chooses the smallest number of candidate subsets,
with a fixed tie convention; no completeness claim depends on that
choice. A candidate is a permitted unused neighbour of degree less
than three for which the edge test passes. If fewer than
$3-\deg(x)$ candidates remain, return. Otherwise enumerate every subset
of that size, in increasing candidate order, adding its edges with the
same necessary tests. Mark $x$ closed and recurse. Each recursive step
saturates another vertex, so the recursion is finite. All degrees,
adjacencies, pair counts and closure marks are restored on return.

Any completion contains one of the enumerated neighbour subsets of
$x$. None of its edges fails the monotone tests, so induction proves
that the plain recursion visits every completion. A terminal graph
is accepted precisely when every degree is three and every neighbour
pair has count one. By~\eqref{pair} this is the required edge-octagon
condition. No assumed graph automorphism group or connectivity test
is used to discard a completion.

The other versions use separately reviewed symmetry lemmas. Every
supplied permutation is checked to be a literal bijection preserving
the initial graph and the entire set of permitted edges.
In the edge-order version, present edges are fixed bits one; absent
pairs touching saturated or closed vertices are fixed bits zero; all
other bits are unknown. A comparison with a permuted state stops at
its first unknown bit, or rejects at its first fixed unequal pair when
the original bit is larger. The least full graph in any orbit survives
at every stage, regardless of the later vertex choices.

The neighbour-orbit version removes that global comparison. At the
chosen vertex $x$, it keeps only supplied permutations fixing $x$ and
preserving the actual candidate set, adjacency, closure marks and
recorded pair-count support. Such permutations preserve the full current
completion problem. Among the possible neighbour subsets it keeps
those not larger than their images in the fixed label order.
Every orbit has a least subset; transporting a completion to that
subset and applying induction preserves existence. A full automorphism
group is unnecessary. The global comparison is not simultaneously
applied, so two unrelated choices of representative cannot conflict.

\section*{Finite certificate and conclusion}
The historical executions in the table use the unchanged recursion and
one of the two exact, reviewed symmetry interfaces. All nine have a
complete final summary, zero accepted graphs, a successful exit status,
and empty standard error. Earlier timed-out computations are preserved
as failures and are not counted. The checker binds each returned row
to its literal input and executed program; the nine rows are disjoint
and exhaust the complete exterior list. Its default replay recompiles
these exact three programs and repeats the nine complete calls. An
explicit recorded-exhaustion mode acquires their closed execution
records while repeating the other checks.

Additional checks compare the actual edge test, pair-count updates and
rollback with an independent graph oracle on subgraphs of the source's
64-vertex positive witness. Three restrictions of that same witness
exercise successful full completions. Exact input checks cover all
literal root and forest edges, all permitted pairs and every supplied
map. Damaged receipts, malformed inputs and optimized Python execution
are rejected. These checks supplement the coverage proof above; source
counts and agreement between implementations are not coverage premises.

No forest admits a completion. Hence order 48 is impossible, and the
radius-four bound and divisibility prove $n(3,8,2)\ge56$.
This recovers one previously stated lower-bound clause. The source's
64-vertex graph is used as a positive control, not claimed as a new
construction or a proof of minimality. No exact count at 56, open
conjecture, historical priority or complete-source recovery is asserted.
\end{document}
